'''John Kevin Stitt''' (born December 28, 1972) is an American businessman and politician serving as the 28th [[governor of Oklahoma]] since 2019. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he was elected in 2018 and reelected in 2022.

Stitt grew up in [[Norman, Oklahoma]], and in 1996 graduated with a bachelor's degree in accounting from [[Oklahoma State University]], where he was a member of the [[Beta Theta Pi]] fraternity. He is the founder and former chairman and CEO of Gateway Mortgage Group. Stitt ran for governor in the [[2018 Oklahoma gubernatorial election|2018 election]], defeating [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] and former [[Attorney General of Oklahoma|state Attorney General]] [[Drew Edmondson]] with 54.3% of the vote. Stitt was reelected to a second term in the [[2022 Oklahoma gubernatorial election|2022 election]], defeating [[Oklahoma Superintendent of Public Instruction|Superintendent of Public Instruction]] [[Joy Hofmeister]], a Republican turned Democrat, with 55.4% of the vote.

==Early life==
John Kevin Stitt was born in [[Milton, Florida]], on December 28, 1972, to Reverend John L. Stitt and Joyce Stitt.<ref name="EOHC">{{cite web |last1=Wilson |first1=Linda D. |title=Stitt, John Kevin (1972– ). |url=https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=ST064 |website=okhistory.org |publisher=[[Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture]] |access-date=25 September 2024 |date=February 11, 2021}}</ref> His mother is of [[Cherokee]] descent and a citizen of the [[Cherokee Nation]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://cqrcengage.com/uwls/app/candidate/100099;jsessionid=q8kglmiuxvkyk93y5kodakci|title=Candidate Profile: Kevin Stitt (R-OK) |website=United Way of Lake and Sumter Counties |access-date=May 8, 2019}}</ref><ref name="auto4">{{cite news |last1=Casteel |first1=Chris |title="Serious kid" sets sights on serving as chief executive of Oklahoma |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/columns/2018/09/30/serious-kid-sets-sights-on-serving-as-chief-executive-of-oklahoma/60498279007/ |access-date=4 October 2022 |work=[[The Oklahoman]] |date=30 September 2018}}</ref> His family moved to [[Skiatook, Oklahoma]], when he was five. He began school in [[Wayne, Oklahoma]], and the family later moved to [[Norman, Oklahoma]], where his father was the pastor of Riverside Church.<ref name="auto4" /> He grew up alongside cousins who participated in [[All Indian Rodeo Cowboys Association]], and has expressed pride in his heritage.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2024/08/12/how-tribal-nations-are-reclaiming-oklahoma|title=How Tribal Nations Are Reclaiming Oklahoma |website=New Yorker|date=5 August 2024 |access-date=August 9, 2024}}</ref> He graduated from [[Norman High School]] and from [[Oklahoma State University]] with a bachelor's degree in accounting in 1996. Stitt is a member of the [[Beta Theta Pi]] fraternity.<ref name="EOHC" />

==Financial services career==
Stitt started his career working for the [[Southwestern Company]], a door-to-door educational bookseller. He later moved to Tulsa, where he met his wife [[Sarah Hazen]]. The couple founded Gateway Mortgage in 2000.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/elections/businessman-kevin-stitt-trying-to-close-the-deal-on-republican/article_fb2e62f0-7ab1-5d3f-ad7e-79ec7a3cc298.html |title=Businessman Kevin Stitt trying to close the deal on Republican gubernatorial nomination |first=Randy |last=Krehbiel |website=Tulsa World |date=August 18, 2018 |access-date=December 6, 2018}}</ref> Stitt was president and CEO until January 2014, when he became chairman-CEO.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/profiles/people/17662028-kevin-stitt|title=Kevin Stitt Profile|website=Bloomberg|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181018203105/https://www.bloomberg.com/profiles/people/17662028-kevin-stitt|archive-date=October 18, 2018|url-status=live|access-date=December 6, 2018}}</ref> He has said he started Gateway in 2000 with "$1,000 and a computer."<ref name="auto4" />

In August 2018, after winning the Republican nomination, Stitt stepped down as Gateway CEO as the company announced a merger with a state-licensed bank and sought its banking license.<ref>{{cite web|last=Brewer|first=Kristina|date=2018-08-03|title=Gateway Mortgage Group Announces New CEO|url=https://dsnews.com/headline/08-03-2018/gateway-hires-ceo|access-date=2020-09-30|website=DSNews|language=en-US}}</ref> Legal Counsel Scott Gesell became CEO in 2020 and Stitt remained chairman.<ref>{{cite web|date=2020-08-25|title=Scott Gesell Named Chief Executive Officer and General Counsel of Gateway First Bank|url=https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20200825005192/en/Scott-Gesell-Named-Chief-Executive-Officer-and-General-Counsel-of-Gateway-First-Bank|access-date=2021-05-26|website=www.businesswire.com|language=en}}</ref> Gateway is a midsize company based in [[Jenks, Oklahoma]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/research/stocks/private/snapshot.asp?privcapId=32365055|title=Company Overview of Gateway Mortgage Group, LLC|website=Bloomberg|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181016131428/https://www.bloomberg.com/research/stocks/private/snapshot.asp?privcapId=32365055|archive-date=October 16, 2018|url-status=live|access-date=April 5, 2019}}</ref>

===Gateway Mortgage license===
After a decade of rapid growth, a few Gateway employees were fired for making non-compliant loans.<ref name="auto2">{{cite web |url=https://newsok.com/article/5605203/a-look-at-cornetts-past-comments-about-trump-and-stitts-mortgage-company-violations |title="A Look At Cornett's Past Comments" Oklahoman, August 19, 2018 |date=August 19, 2018 |access-date=October 27, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180928121733/https://newsok.com/article/5605203/a-look-at-cornetts-past-comments-about-trump-and-stitts-mortgage-company-violations |archive-date=September 28, 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2009, Gateway was listed in a ''[[Business Insider]]'' article as one of the 15 shadiest lenders in the government-backed mortgage industry.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/the-shady-lenders-abusing-government-mortgage-problems-2009-12|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024112854/https://www.businessinsider.com/the-shady-lenders-abusing-government-mortgage-problems-2009-12?op=1|title=The 15 Shadiest Mortgage Lenders Being Backed By The Government|first=Vincent|last=Fernando|archive-date=October 24, 2018|website=Business Insider}}</ref><ref name=truth-test>{{Cite web|url=https://www.news9.com/story/5e348c34527dcf49dad7b4b7/truth-test:-kevin-stitts-business-record|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181018131231/http://www.news9.com/story/38458020/truth-test-kevin-stitts-business-record|title=Truth Test: Kevin Stitt's Business Record|first=David|last=Griffin|archive-date=October 18, 2018|website=www.news9.com}}</ref> The article said Gateway originated nearly twice as many bad mortgages as its competitors.<ref name=truth-test/> An August 19, 2018, ''Oklahoman'' newspaper article highlighted the ''Business Insider'' article's inaccuracies, reporting that "in the Illinois case, a consent order states that the Illinois banking agency investigated a Gateway loan originator for an 'alleged real estate, appraisal, and mortgage fraud scheme.' Gateway fired the employee, asked for a hearing and then agreed to what investigators found. Gateway agreed to a $10,000 fine. The Stitt campaign responded with a press release that said, "the license in Illinois was never revoked. The state agreed after the appeal not to revoke the license."<ref name="auto2"/>

[[KWTV-DT|KWTV]] also said that according to Georgia's Department of Banking and Finance, Stitt was banned for five years and the company was banned for life from origination mortgages in Georgia. According to the ''Oklahoman'', a Gateway corporate attorney said there were misrepresentations and insufficient background checks by employees in the Georgia office but Stitt was not involved. The employees were fired and Gateway paid a $2,000 fine. The state overturned the lifetime ban on Gateway, effective November 2017. Gateway is able to do business in all 50 states.<ref name="auto2"/>

During Stitt's gubernatorial campaign, ''[[Oklahoma Watch]]'' reported that Wisconsin regulators fined Gateway for a "clerical error" regarding its history with regulators from other states. Gateway corrected the application and was issued a license in 2009. It remains in good standing in Wisconsin.<ref>{{cite web | last1=Monies | first1=Paul | url=https://oklahomawatch.org/2018/08/16/stitts-mortgage-firm-failed-to-tell-regulators-of-past-problems/ | title=Stitt's Mortgage Firm Failed to Tell Regulators of Past Problems | work=Oklahoma Watch | date=August 17, 2018 | access-date=October 16, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181016164901/https://oklahomawatch.org/2018/08/16/stitts-mortgage-firm-failed-to-tell-regulators-of-past-problems/ | archive-date=October 16, 2018 | url-status=live }}</ref>

==Governor of Oklahoma==
===2018 election===
{{main|2018 Oklahoma gubernatorial election}}

In July 2017, Stitt announced his candidacy<ref>Kevin Stitt to run for governor, Tulsa Beacon, "Tulsa businessman Kevin Stitt announced his candidacy for governor in 2018. The first-time candidate</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.realclearpolitics.com/articles/2018/08/24/collapse_of_the_career_politicians_137882.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180825113644/https://www.realclearpolitics.com/articles/2018/08/24/collapse_of_the_career_politicians_137882.html|title=Real Clear Politics, By Erick Erickson, August 24, 2018, Collapse of the Career Politicians, "The prevailing consensus among political analysts in the United States about..."|archive-date=August 25, 2018}}</ref><ref>U.S. News & World Report, Mortgage Company Head Wins GOP Primary for Oklahoma Governor, "Tulsa mortgage company owner and political newcomer Kevin Stitt won the Republican nomination in the race to become Oklahoma's next governor.", August 28, 2018,</ref> for the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nomination<ref>[https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2018/live-updates/midterms/live-primary-election-results/lieutenant-governor-fails-to-make-republican-gubernatorial-runoff-in-oklahoma/ ''Washington Post'', June 27, 2018, by Terri Rupar, "Lieutenant governor concedes in GOP gubernatorial race in Oklahoma; runoff set"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190107072508/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2018/live-updates/midterms/live-primary-election-results/lieutenant-governor-fails-to-make-republican-gubernatorial-runoff-in-oklahoma/ |date=January 7, 2019 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fox23.com/news/you-decide-2018-oklahoma-primary-elections/776924575|title=You Decide 2018: Kevin Stitt wins Oklahoma gubernatorial GOP runoff race|last=EndPlay|date=August 29, 2018|website=KOKI|access-date=December 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181016164930/https://www.fox23.com/news/you-decide-2018-oklahoma-primary-elections/776924575|archive-date=October 16, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> for governor in 2018.<ref>{{cite web|last=Greenwood|first=Max|date=2018-08-30|title=Trump endorses Kevin Stitt in Oklahoma governor's race|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/404429-trump-endorses-kevin-stitt-in-oklahoma-governors-race|access-date=2020-09-30|website=The Hill|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/tulsa-businessman-political-novice-kevin-stitt-wins-gop-primary-for-oklahoma-governor|title=Tulsa businessman, political novice Kevin Stitt wins GOP primary for Oklahoma governor|first=Frank|last=Miles|date=August 28, 2018|website=Fox News|access-date=December 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128123912/https://www.foxnews.com/politics/tulsa-businessman-political-novice-kevin-stitt-wins-gop-primary-for-oklahoma-governor|archive-date=November 28, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> Facing nine other candidates in the primary election, he ran a statewide campaign with stops in nearly every city and town in all 77 counties. He finished second, defeating, among others, Lieutenant Governor [[Todd Lamb (politician)|Todd Lamb]].<ref>KFOR, August 29, 2018, Kevin Stitt, Drew Edmondson, Chris Powell to face off in November as gubernatorial candidates</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kxii.com/content/news/Oklahoma-governors-race-tops-primary-runoff-election-ballot-491900101.html|title=Kevin Stitt wins GOP nod for Oklahoma governor|last=KXII|website=www.kxii.com|date=August 28, 2018 |access-date=December 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024073908/https://www.kxii.com/content/news/Oklahoma-governors-race-tops-primary-runoff-election-ballot-491900101.html|archive-date=October 24, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> In the August 28 primary runoff, Stitt defeated [[Mick Cornett]], a former mayor of Oklahoma City.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.fox23.com/news/you-decide-2018-oklahoma-primary-elections/776924575| title=You Decide 2018: Kevin Stitt wins Oklahoma gubernatorial GOP runoff race| date=August 29, 2018| access-date=October 16, 2018| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181016164930/https://www.fox23.com/news/you-decide-2018-oklahoma-primary-elections/776924575| archive-date=October 16, 2018| url-status=live}}</ref> In the November general election, Stitt defeated the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee, former [[Attorney General of Oklahoma|attorney general]] [[Drew Edmondson]], and [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] [[Chris Powell (politician)|Chris Powell]].<ref name="EOHC" />

In the GOP runoff, political newcomer Stitt received crucial support from a trio of conservative leaders: U.S. Senator [[Ted Cruz]]<ref>{{cite web |title=Senator Ted Cruz endorses Kevin Stitt for governor |url=https://ktul.com/news/beyond-the-podium/senator-ted-cruz-endorses-kevin-stitt-for-governor |access-date=August 22, 2018 |date=August 22, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180823042034/https://ktul.com/news/beyond-the-podium/senator-ted-cruz-endorses-kevin-stitt-for-governor |archive-date=August 23, 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> and former U.S. senators [[Rick Santorum]]<ref>{{cite web |title=Rick Santorum Endorsement |website = [[YouTube]]|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XgB546wLEHI |access-date=August 17, 2018 |date=August 6, 2018}}</ref> and [[Tom Coburn]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Tom Coburn endorses Stitt for governor |url=http://www.normantranscript.com/news/tom-coburn-endorses-stitt-for-governor/article_fd08ff14-a560-11e8-8ff2-0b7b5a0eeedc.html |access-date=August 22, 2018|date=August 21, 2018}}</ref> In the general election, Stitt was endorsed by former primary rival Mick Cornett,<ref name="auto">{{cite web|date=2018-10-11|title=Stitt welcomes Cornett's endorsement but not Fallin's|url=https://oklahoman.com/article/5611359/stitt-welcomes-cornetts-endorsement-but-not-fallins/|access-date=2020-09-30|website=Oklahoman.com|language=en-US}}</ref> incumbent governor of Oklahoma [[Mary Fallin]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.news9.com/story/39276503/gov-mary-fallin-endorses-republican-gubernatorial-candidate-kevin-stitt|title=Gov. Mary Fallin Endorses Republican Gubernatorial Candidate Kevin Stitt|website=www.news9.com|access-date=December 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190115032750/http://www.news9.com/story/39276503/gov-mary-fallin-endorses-republican-gubernatorial-candidate-kevin-stitt|archive-date=January 15, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> and President [[Donald Trump]].<ref>{{cite web| url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/404429-trump-endorses-kevin-stitt-in-oklahoma-governors-race| title=Trump endorses Kevin Stitt in Oklahoma governor's race| date=August 30, 2018| access-date=October 16, 2018| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181016164903/https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/404429-trump-endorses-kevin-stitt-in-oklahoma-governors-race| archive-date=October 16, 2018| url-status=live}}</ref>

The Stitt campaign responded to Fallin's endorsement with a press release: "We did not seek [Fallin's endorsement], and Kevin Stitt has run on a campaign message that he will do things a lot differently. He is focused on changing the structure of state government and cleaning up the mess we are currently in at the Capitol."<ref name="auto"/>

During his campaign, Stitt called himself "the only job creator with proven business experience" running for governor<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.muskogeephoenix.com/news/quick-gop-gubernatorial-candidate-stakes-out-positions/article_e0fd51cf-84cc-5e63-9651-9a44be3e20da.html|title=Quick 5: GOP gubernatorial candidate stakes out positions|work=Muskogee Phoenix|access-date=October 27, 2018|language=en}}</ref> and emphasized his business background.<ref name="auto3">{{Cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/elections/republican-candidate-for-governor-kevin-stitt-says-oklahoma-could-be/article_8d5e3f24-ee44-5934-ac15-bad313a1dd45.html|title=Republican candidate for governor Kevin Stitt says Oklahoma could be a 'top 10 state'|last=World|first=Randy Krehbiel Tulsa|work=Tulsa World|access-date=October 27, 2018|language=en}}</ref> He called on the state to become "top 10 in job growth, top 10 in education and top 10 in infrastructure."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.claremoreprogress.com/news/stitt-oklahoma-could-be-a-top-state/article_0683b6e0-cd9a-11e8-b6f4-13a3a189c8c6.html|title=STITT: Oklahoma could be a top 10 state|first=Kayleigh|last=Thesenvitz|work=Claremore Daily Progress|access-date=October 27, 2018|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181028033708/https://www.claremoreprogress.com/news/stitt-oklahoma-could-be-a-top-state/article_0683b6e0-cd9a-11e8-b6f4-13a3a189c8c6.html|archive-date=October 28, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

During the general election, the close race drew increased attention from national media and political figures.<ref>The Journal Record, (Mike Simons/Tulsa World via AP), Pence campaigns for Stitt in Tulsa, Vice President Mike Pence speaks at a rally for gubernatorial candidate Kevin Stitt at the Mabee Center in Tulsa on Thursday. With the general election less than three weeks away, Kevin Stitt is trying to get by with a little help from his friends.</ref> Vice President [[Mike Pence]] campaigned for Stitt.<ref>U.S. News & World Report, October 18, 2018, VP Mike Pence Campaigns for GOP's Kevin Stitt for Governor
"Vice President Mike Pence headlined a campaign rally for Republican Kevin Stitt as they worked to keep the Oklahoma governor's office in GOP hands."</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.theokie.com/tulsa-world-kevin-stitt-a-better-agent-of-change/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024112954/http://www.theokie.com/tulsa-world-kevin-stitt-a-better-agent-of-change/|title=The Okie » Tulsa World: Kevin Stitt, A Better Agent of Change|date=October 2018 |archive-date=October 24, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/28406c5caa1d424ba58495ea5d15f66b|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024112918/https://apnews.com/28406c5caa1d424ba58495ea5d15f66b|title=The Latest: Oklahoma attorney general race too close to call|date=August 29, 2018|archive-date=October 24, 2018|website=AP NEWS}}</ref>

===2022 election===
{{main|2022 Oklahoma gubernatorial election}}

Stitt filed to run for reelection in January 2021.<ref name="Stitt Announce">{{cite web |title=Gov. Kevin Stitt Files Paperwork To Run For Re-Election in 2022 |url=https://www.news9.com/story/600235973972130be65f3fd4/gov-kevin-stitt-files-paperwork-to-run-for-reelection-in-2022 |website=News 9 |language=en |date=January 15, 2021}}</ref> He won the Republican primary in June 2022 and was reelected in November.<ref name="Governor Nominees">{{cite news |last1=DenHoed |first1=Andrea |title=Kevin Stitt, Joy Hofmeister advance in governor's race |url=https://nondoc.com/2022/06/28/kevin-stitt-joy-hofmeister-advance-in-governor-race/ |access-date=29 June 2022 |publisher=NonDoc |date=28 June 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hoberock |first1=Barbara |title=Gov. Kevin Stitt wins second term |url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/local/gov-kevin-stitt-wins-second-term/article_1b85f720-5ed1-11ed-b72b-57470d0a0d6f.html |access-date=9 November 2022 |work=[[Tulsa World]] |date=8 November 2022}}</ref>

===Tenure===
[[File:President Donald J. Trump Meets with Governors-Elect (32440546418).jpg|thumb|300px|Stitt (left) attending a [[White House]] conference in December 2018, seated next to Governor-elect [[Brad Little]] of [[Idaho]]]]Stitt was inaugurated on January 14, 2019, at the [[Oklahoma State Capitol]]. Chief Justice of Oklahoma [[Noma Gurich]] swore him and [[Lieutenant Governor of Oklahoma|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Matt Pinnell]] into office. Stitt gave a 15-minute inaugural address.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsok.com/article/5620223/stitt-takes-office-promises-new-state-pride|title=Stitt takes office, promises new state pride|last=Felder|first=Ben|work=The Daily Oklahoman|date=January 14, 2019|access-date=January 15, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190114235712/https://newsok.com/article/5620223/stitt-takes-office-promises-new-state-pride|archive-date=January 14, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

