Before taking office, Stitt nominated former state representative [[Michael Rogers (Oklahoma politician)|Michael Rogers]] as his [[Oklahoma Secretary of State|secretary of state]] and Tulsa deputy mayor Michael Junk (a former advisor to U.S. senators Jim Inhofe and Tom Coburn)<ref>{{cite web|last=World|first=Randy Krehbiel Tulsa|title=People to Watch: As chief of staff, Michael Junk's political career is on the rise|url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/local/people-to-watch-as-chief-of-staff-michael-junks-political-career-is-on-the-rise/article_7e90aeef-4f93-5f66-96a0-1079e63b5377.html|access-date=2020-09-30|website=Tulsa World|date=January 5, 2019 |language=en}}</ref> as his chief of staff.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/government/tulsa-deputy-mayor-michael-junk-picked-to-be-gov-/article_cb5fa449-61d2-5956-b087-e7caf90a2ffb.html|title=Tulsa Deputy Mayor Michael Junk picked to be Gov.-elect Kevin Stitt's chief of staff|last=Hoberock|first=Barbara|work=Tulsa World|date=November 27, 2018}}</ref>

On December 23, 2019, citing disagreements with Stitt over his handling of negotiations with the state's various Indian tribes about gambling compacts, [[Lisa Johnson Billy]] became the first member of Stitt's cabinet to resign. A member of the [[Chickasaw Nation]] and former Republican state representative, Billy viewed Stitt's negotiation position as one of "unnecessary conflict."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://nondoc.com/2019/12/23/lisa-billy-resigns-as-gov-stitts-secretary-of-native-american-affairs/|title=Lisa Billy resigns as Gov. Stitt's secretary of Native American affairs|work=NonDoc Media|first=Tres|last=Savage|date=December 23, 2019}}</ref> Stitt tapped his secretary of state Mike Rogers to assume those duties and temporarily combined the two positions.

