===Abortion===
In April 2022, Stitt supported, and signed into law, SB 612, which makes performing an abortion a crime punishable by 10 years in prison or a $100,000 fine, with exceptions for medical emergencies but none for rape or incest.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Paul|last1=LeBlanc|first2=Veronica|last2=Stracqualursi|title=Oklahoma governor signs near-total ban on abortion into law |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/12/politics/oklahoma-abortion-ban-kevin-stitt/index.html |access-date=2022-05-06 |website=CNN}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |website=[[Center for Reproductive Rights]] |date=25 May 2022 |title=Oklahoma Becomes First State to Entirely Ban Abortion |url=https://reproductiverights.org/oklahoma-becomes-first-state-to-entirely-ban-abortion/}}</ref> Later in May, Stitt signed into law an even more restrictive bill, House Bill 4327, "banning abortions from the stage of 'fertilization' and allowing private citizens to sue abortion providers who 'knowingly' perform or induce an abortion 'on a pregnant woman.'" Abortion in cases of rape, incest, or high-risk pregnancies will continue to be permitted.<ref>{{cite web |last=O'Neil |first=Tyler |date=2022-05-25 |title=Oklahoma abortion law protecting life from conception to face legal challenge from Planned Parenthood |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/oklahoma-abortion-law-conception-legal-challenge-planned-parenthood |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=Fox News |language=en-US}}</ref> It is the most restrictive ban on elective abortion in the United States.<ref>{{Cite news |date=26 May 2022 |title=Oklahoma GOP governor signs one of nation's strictest abortion bills into law |work=CNN |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/25/politics/oklahoma-abortion-ban-hb-4327-signed-governor-stitt/index.html}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=26 May 2022 |title=Oklahoma Governor Signs Bill That Bans Most Abortions |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/25/us/oklahoma-abortion-ban-law-governor.html}}</ref> The ACLU announced that it would fight the ban in court.<ref>{{Cite news |date=20 May 2022 |title=Lawsuit planned over 'extreme,' 'very dangerous' latest abortion ban, ACLU Oklahoma says |work=KOCO |url=https://www.koco.com/article/oklahoma-abortion-ban-lawsuit-aclu-capitol-stitt-conception/40063375}}</ref>

===Capital punishment===
Oklahoma has a [[Capital punishment in Oklahoma|long history]] with [[capital punishment]], having conducted the second-most executions since the death penalty was reinstated in ''[[Gregg v. Georgia]]'' (1976).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/executions/executions-overview/number-of-executions-by-state-and-region-since-1976|title=Executions by State and Region Since 1976|website=Death Penalty Information Center|access-date=December 11, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191210062311/https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/executions/executions-overview/number-of-executions-by-state-and-region-since-1976|archive-date=December 10, 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref> But in 2015, a moratorium was placed on all state executions following the [[execution of Clayton Lockett|botched execution of Clayton Lockett]] in April 2014 and the execution of Charles Warner by unauthorized methods in January 2015.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/crime-courts/oklahoma-set-resume-executions-years-after-death-chamber-mishaps-n1136821|title=Oklahoma set to resume executions years after death-chamber mishaps|work=NBC News|agency=Associated Press|date=February 13, 2020}}</ref> On February 13, 2020, Stitt announced that the moratorium would be lifted and executions resumed under his tenure.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://nondoc.com/2020/02/13/lethal-announcement-oklahoma-to-resume-executions-this-year/|title=Lethal announcement: Oklahoma to resume executions this year
|work=NonDoc Media|first=Matt|last=Patterson|date=February 13, 2020}}</ref>

Throughout his governorship, Stitt, a strong supporter of capital punishment, had granted clemency twice to inmates facing imminent execution in Oklahoma. On November 18, 2021, he commuted the death sentence of [[Julius Jones (prisoner)|Julius Jones]] to life without the possibility of parole, hours before Jones was originally due to be executed.<ref>{{cite news|last=Murphy|first=Sean|date=November 18, 2021|title=EXPLAINER: Julius Jones' execution is stopped, with clemency|work=Associated Press News|url=https://apnews.com/article/entertainment-shootings-executions-oklahoma-oklahoma-city-9cfa139d45b79e72918a8841e3f2250b|access-date=November 18, 2021}}</ref> On November 13, 2025, minutes before the scheduled execution of Tremane Wood, Stitt commuted Wood's death sentence to life imprisonment without parole.<ref>{{cite news|date=November 13, 2025 |title=Oklahoma inmate's life spared moments before scheduled lethal injection |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/13/tremane-wood-clemency-oklahoma }}</ref>

===Criminal justice and mass incarceration ===

==== Oklahoma Pardon and Parole Board ====
Three of the five [[Oklahoma Pardon and Parole Board]] members are appointed by the governor. They serve four year terms that run concurrent with the governor's.<ref>{{Cite news |date=25 May 2022 |title=Stitt wants statement that he put 'improper political pressure' on Parole Board appointees stricken from grand jury report |work=KPVI |url=https://www.kpvi.com/news/national_news/stitt-wants-statement-that-he-put-improper-political-pressure-on-parole-board-appointees-stricken-from/article_4f5b73e4-a96c-5957-a808-5ee1b75e36d6.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526073459/https://www.kpvi.com/news/national_news/stitt-wants-statement-that-he-put-improper-political-pressure-on-parole-board-appointees-stricken-from/article_4f5b73e4-a96c-5957-a808-5ee1b75e36d6.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=May 26, 2022 }}</ref> Before Adam Luck and Kelley Doyle were pressured to resign from the Board in 2022, Stitt had expressed full confidence in the board over criticisms from [[District attorney|district attorneys]] like [[Steve Kunzweiler]] who want the board to be more conservative in their considerations for parole and commutation. The ''Tulsa World'' reported that the district attorneys were taking an increasingly more political role that has "to some degree weakened" the board's influence.<ref>{{Cite news |date=5 December 2021 |title=Stitt expresses confidence in Pardon and Parole Board |work=Tulsa World |url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/stitt-expresses-confidence-in-pardon-and-parole-board/article_a4bba9e0-52de-11ec-b0d6-efa4acfddac7.html}}</ref> This came at the same time that [[dark money]] conservative advertisements targeting Stitt as not tough enough on crime began to air.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2 December 2021 |title=Dark money group targets Oklahoma Gov. Kevin Stitt with Fox News attack ads |work=Tulsa World |url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/dark-money-group-targets-oklahoma-gov-kevin-stitt-with-fox-news-attack-ads/article_6f5a7810-5371-11ec-a476-f3808fe4da13.html}}</ref> All of this plays out despite Oklahoma incarcerating a "higher percentage of its people than any democracy on earth."<ref>{{Cite news |title=Oklahoma profile |work=Prison Policy Initiative |url=https://www.prisonpolicy.org/profiles/OK.html#:~:text=Oklahoma%20has%20an%20incarceration%20rate,incarcerated%20in%20Oklahoma%20and%20why.}}</ref> According to Prison Policy Initiative, Oklahoma had the third-highest incarceration rate in 2021, and in 2018, it incarcerated the most women per capita.<ref>{{Cite news |title=States of Incarceration: The Global Context 2021 |work=Prison Policy |url=https://www.prisonpolicy.org/global/2021.html}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Kajstura |first=Aleks |title=States of Women's Incarceration: The Global Context 2018 |url=https://www.prisonpolicy.org/global/women/2018.html}}</ref>

In 2022, Stitt at first agreed to grant parole to Jimmie Stohler, the Crossbow Killer, after a recommendation from the Oklahoma Pardon and Parole Board, but later rescinded his decision.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2022 |title=Ex-Tulsa officer convicted in crossbow, poison arrow murder gets parole revoked |work=KJRH |url=https://www.kjrh.com/news/local-news/ex-tulsa-officer-convicted-in-crossbow-poison-arrow-murder-gets-parole}}</ref>

Findings in a 2022 [[grand jury]] report filed by [[David Prater (attorney)|David Prater]] criticized Stitt for being grossly improper, claimed that he pressured the Oklahoma Pardon and Parole Board, and that his private meetings seem to have violated the Open Meetings Act.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Grand jury criticizes Pardon and Parole Board, governor over commutations |work=KPVI |url=https://www.kpvi.com/news/national_news/grand-jury-criticizes-pardon-and-parole-board-governor-over-commutations/article_5619fa4a-b979-5fc2-9403-27286c4fe809.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513030010/https://www.kpvi.com/news/national_news/grand-jury-criticizes-pardon-and-parole-board-governor-over-commutations/article_5619fa4a-b979-5fc2-9403-27286c4fe809.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=May 13, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=Grand jury report criticizes Pardon and Parole board members, state leaders |work=2 News Oklahoma |url=https://www.kjrh.com/news/local-news/grand-jury-report-criticizes-pardon-and-parole-board-members-state-leaders}}</ref> DAs have the ability to bring grand juries.<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 May 2022 |title=Long Story Short: What's Behind A Grand Jury's Rebuke of the Governor, Pardon and Parole Board |work=[[Oklahoma Watch]] |url=https://oklahomawatch.org/2022/05/24/long-story-short-whats-behind-a-grand-jurys-rebuke-of-the-governor-pardon-and-parole-board/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=12 May 2022 |title=Governor, legislative leaders in crosshairs of Oklahoma County grand jury report |url=https://nondoc.com/2022/05/12/oklahoma-county-grand-jury-report/}}</ref> When the report came out, Stitt's office issued a statement saying, "This is the latest in a string of unfounded hit jobs by the Oklahoma County District Attorney and other political insiders."<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 May 2022 |title=Grand jury criticizes Pardon and Parole Board, governor over commutations |work=Tulsa World |url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/grand-jury-criticizes-pardon-and-parole-board-governor-over-commutations/article_7aac9d08-d21d-11ec-88c6-abe9d2d184b5.html}}</ref> A spokesman for Stitt said, "Oklahoma law explicitly prohibits grand juries from making allegations that public officials have engaged in misconduct, and it is clear the outgoing prosecutor took advantage of the citizens who served on this grand jury to unwittingly carry out his partisan feud against Governor Stitt and the Pardon and Parole Board."<ref>{{Cite news |date=13 May 2022 |title=Grand jury report criticizes Oklahoma governor, parole board |newspaper=Washington Post |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/grand-jury-report-criticizes-oklahoma-governor-parole-board/2022/05/13/8a1b5760-d2ff-11ec-886b-df76183d233f_story.html}}</ref> The report noted that the jury "had no legal authority to accuse the governor of official misconduct, which can only be done in impeachment proceedings."<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 May 2022 |title=Grand Jury Criticizes Gov. Kevin Stitt, Pardon and Parole Board Over Commutation Processes |work=[[Oklahoma Watch]] |url=https://oklahomawatch.org/2022/05/12/grand-jury-criticizes-gov-kevin-stitt-pardon-and-parole-board-over-commutation-processes-%EF%BF%BC/}}</ref> Later, Stitt "asked a judge to strike from a grand jury report a finding that he placed 'improper political pressure' on his appointees to the Oklahoma Pardon and Parole Board."

==== Legislation ====
Stitt attempted to address the state's overincarceration crisis. Beginning with the adoption of State Question 780 by Oklahoma voters in 2016, advocates for criminal justice reform sought additional measures. SQ780, which changed the classification of simple drug possession crimes from felony to misdemeanor and increased the cap for property crimes to be considered felonies, had already reduced the rate of felony prosecution statewide by 26% by 2018.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://okpolicy.org/sq-780-already-reshaping-oklahomas-justice-system/|title=SQ 780 is already reshaping Oklahoma's justice system|last=Gentzler|first=Ryan|date=February 19, 2018}}</ref> In May 2019, Stitt proposed several ideas, including making SQ780's sentencing standards retroactive, prohibiting criminal records from being considered for professional licensing, and restructuring the funding scheme for the various district attorney offices.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/local/government-and-politics/gov-kevin-stitt-rolls-out-new-criminal-justice-reform-package/article_1f514e08-3493-5be9-91f8-8d18f1e81f26.html|title=Gov. Kevin Stitt rolls out new criminal justice reform package |last=Haberock|first=Barbara|date=May 2, 2018}}</ref> The legislature made SQ780 retroactive by allowing parole for those convicted before SQ780 became effective and reforming professional licensing,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/local/government-and-politics/oklahoma-watch-questions-answered-on-this-year-s-criminal-justice/article_e2b02edb-7cac-52c7-bb1e-c125a68cc89b.html|title=Oklahoma Watch: 5 questions answered on this year's criminal justice reform bills |last=Monies|first=Paul|date=April 29, 2019}}</ref> but did not approve bills to reform Oklahoma's cash [[bail]] system.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://stateimpact.npr.org/oklahoma/2019/05/30/legislators-leave-criminal-justice-reform-bills-on-the-table-as-prison-population-grows/|title=Legislators leave criminal justice reform bills on the table as prison population grows|last=Chandler|first=Quinton|date=May 30, 2019}}</ref> In response to legislative defeats, Stitt issued an [https://www.sos.ok.gov/documents/executive/1874.pdf executive order] to form a study group to make recommendations for future criminal justice reform for consideration during the 2020 legislative session, with particular emphasis on reducing Oklahoma's incarceration rate.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.governor.ok.gov/articles/pressreleases/governor-stitt-issues-executive-order-to-create|title=Governor Stitt issues Executive order to Create RESTORE Task Force|last=Office of Governor Stitt|date=May 20, 2019}}</ref>

In mid-2018, Oklahoma voters approved State Question 788, which legalized the licensed use, sale, and growth of [[medical marijuana|marijuana for medical purposes]]. As a candidate, Stitt cited a need to implement the results of the election by enacting a comprehensive regulatory scheme.<ref>{{cite news |last=Skarky |first=Brent |date=July 26, 2018 |title=Gubernatorial candidates weigh in on medical marijuana debate |url=https://kfor.com/2018/07/26/gubernatorial-candidates-weigh-in-on-medical-marijuana-debate/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191022233623/https://kfor.com/2018/07/26/gubernatorial-candidates-weigh-in-on-medical-marijuana-debate/ |archive-date=October 22, 2019 |access-date=September 9, 2019 |url-status=dead}}</ref> After months of negotiation with legislative leaders, Stitt signed HB2612, the Oklahoma Medical Marijuana and Patient Protection Act. Also known as the "Marijuana Unity Bill", HB2612 provided an extensive medical marijuana framework, including licensing requirements and rights for patients.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hoberock |first=Barbara |date=March 15, 2019 |title=Medical marijuana 'Unity Bill' signed into law by Gov. Kevin Stitt |url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/medical-marijuana-unity-bill-signed-into-law-by-gov-kevin/article_b5d190a9-c211-5ef9-80c9-60694b8c07d8.html}}</ref>

===Critical race theory===
On May 7, 2021, Stitt signed a bill prohibiting the teaching of [[critical race theory]] or its gender equivalent in public schools.<ref name=":1">{{cite web | last1=Forman | first1=Carmen | title=Stitt signs controversial bill limiting race, gender curriculum in schools | url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/2021/05/07/oklahoma-gov-stitt-signs-bill-censoring-race-gender-school-curriculum/4989720001/ | date=May 7, 2021 | work=[[The Oklahoman]] | access-date=May 8, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Polus | first1=Sarah | title=Oklahoma governor signs bill that prevents schools from teaching critical race theory | url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/552451-oklahoma-governor-signs-bill-that-prevents-schools-from-teaching | date=May 7, 2021 | work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] | access-date=May 8, 2021}}</ref> ''The Oklahoman'' wrote that it was unclear whether critical race theory was taught at any Oklahoma public schools.<ref name=":1" /> Opponents of the bill said it was intended to discourage nuanced discussions about race and whitewash the United States' history on race.<ref name=":1" /> Stitt invoked [[Martin Luther King Jr.]] when he signed the bill.<ref name=":1" />

===LGBT issues===
In November, Stitt issued an executive order that prohibited transgender people from changing the gender on their birth certificates. In 2022, he signed into law a bill prohibiting nonbinary gender markers on birth certificates, saying: "People are created by God to be male or female. There is no such thing as nonbinary sex." Transgender people criticized Stitt, saying it was difficult for them to navigate life when their official documents do not match their gender identity. According to the [[American Medical Association]], "empirical evidence has demonstrated that trans and nonbinary gender identities are normal variations of human identity and expression."<ref>{{Cite news |title=Okla. stakes out new battleground on LGBTQ rights: Birth certificates |language=en-US |newspaper=Washington Post |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/04/30/oklahoma-birth-certificate-ban-nonbinary/ |access-date=2022-04-30 |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

On May 25, 2022, Stitt signed a bill into law that will require students at public charter schools and public schools to use locker rooms and bathrooms that match the sex listed on their birth certificate.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Andy|last1=Rose|first2=Paul|last2=LeBlanc|title=Oklahoma GOP governor signs anti-transgender bathroom bill into law |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/25/politics/oklahoma-anti-transgender-bathroom-law-signed-stitt/index.html |access-date=2022-05-27 |website=CNN}}</ref>

In May 2023, Stitt vetoed funding for Oklahoma's PBS network [[Oklahoma Educational Television Authority|OETA]], accusing it of broadcasting pro-[[LGBT]] content that "indoctrinat[es]" children.<ref>{{Cite web |last=FERGUSON |first=TOM |date=2023-05-03 |title=Minority Leader Munson calls for legislature to override OETA funding bill veto |url=https://okcfox.com/news/local/minority-leader-munson-calls-for-legislature-to-override-oeta-funding-bill-veto-cyndi-oklahoma-city-educational-television-authority-hb-2820-1009-kevin-stitt-governor-democrat-republican-politics-capitol-house-senate-bob-spinks-friends-woke-programming |access-date=2023-05-04 |website=KOKH |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Forman |first=Carmen |title=Stitt vetoes OETA bill, accuses network of trying to 'indoctrinate' kids |url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/stitt-vetoes-oeta-bill-accuses-network-of-trying-to-indoctrinate-kids/article_5fc1a3b8-e5da-11ed-ae48-c31a4283a5d3.html |access-date=2023-05-04 |website=Tulsa World |date=April 28, 2023 |language=en}}</ref> The [[Oklahoma Legislature]] overrode the veto.<ref>{{cite web |title=Bill Information for HB 2820 |url=http://www.oklegislature.gov/BillInfo.aspx?Bill=HB2820&Session=2300 |website=Oklahoma State Legislature |access-date=May 29, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Savage |first1=Tres |title='Tantrum': Legislature overrides 19 Stitt vetoes, but some budget bills hit snag |url=https://nondoc.com/2023/05/26/legislature-overrides-stitt-vetoes-budget-snag/ |website=NonDoc |date=May 26, 2023 |publisher=Sustainable Journalism Foundation |access-date=May 30, 2023}}</ref>

On August 1, 2023, Stitt issued the Women's Bill of Rights by executive order. It defines the words "male" and "female" to mean biological sex at birth.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Stitt Leads The Nation As First Governor To Issue Women's Bill Of Rights By Executive Order |url=https://oklahoma.gov/governor/newsroom/newsroom/2023/august2023/governor-stitt-leads-the-nation-as-first-governor-to-issue-women.html |access-date=2023-10-31 |website=Governor J. Kevin Stitt |language=en}}</ref>

===Gamefowl Commission===
In November 2023, Stitt appeared in a prerecorded video at the [[Oklahoma Gamefowl Commission]]'s annual meeting, saying: "You all know Oklahoma's long and storied history with gamefowl, from statehood to today. Oklahomans like yourselves remain dedicated to the spirit of competition and camaraderie that runs deep in our communities."<ref name="Dulaney-1">{{cite news |last1=Dulaney |first1=Josh |title=Gov. Kevin Stitt under fire for comments that appear to support cockfighting group |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/2023/11/15/oklahoma-gamefowl-commission-governor-kevin-stitt-comments-draw-criticism-cockfighting/71597578007/ |access-date=21 November 2023 |work=[[The Oklahoman]] |date=November 15, 2023}}</ref> The statement was criticized by former governor [[Frank Keating]], former attorney general [[Drew Edmondson]], and [[animal rights]] groups as an endorsement of the commission's efforts to reduce the penalty for [[cockfighting]] in Oklahoma to a misdemeanor.<ref name="Dulaney-1" /> A Stitt spokesperson responded: "The governor of course does not support animal cruelty. He supports Oklahoma agriculture and often records videos for ag groups around the state... [the governor] has not seen or endorsed any legislation on this topic."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dulaney |first1=Josh |title=Governor's office says Stitt's praise of Gamefowl Commission wasn't meant to support animal cruelty |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2023/11/17/oklahoma-governor-kevin-stitt-responds-criticism-oklahoma-gamefowl-commission-cockfighting/71598627007/ |access-date=21 November 2023 |work=[[The Oklahoman]] |date=November 17, 2023}}</ref> [[YouTube]] later took the video down for violating community standards by promoting animal cruelty.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Carter |first1=M. Scott |title=YouTube pulls video of Oklahoma Gov. Stitt praising cockfighting group, cites animal cruelty |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2023/11/20/youtube-pulls-oklahoma-gov-kevin-stitt-video-promoting-cockfighting-industry-cites-animal-cruelty/71655802007/ |access-date=21 November 2023 |work=[[The Oklahoman]] |date=November 20, 2023}}</ref> After Stitt's video message, the Gamefowl Commission asked its members to oppose the nomination of [[Sara E. Hill]] to the federal judiciary, saying, "our governor, Kevin Stitt, has asked that we call and email Senators [[James Lankford|Lankford]] and [[Markwayne Mullin|Mullin]] to rescind their approval of Ms. Sara Hill as a federal judge in Northern Oklahoma."<ref name="McNutt-1">{{cite news |last1=McNutt |first1=Michael |title='A terrible mistake': Barry Switzer urges Gov. Stitt to issue statement opposing cockfighting |url=https://nondoc.com/2023/11/22/a-terrible-mistake-barry-switzer-urges-gov-stitt-to-issue-statement-opposing-cockfighting/ |access-date=24 November 2023 |work=NonDoc |date=22 November 2023}}</ref> Stitt had previously opposed Hill's nomination.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Patterson |first1=Matt |last2=Brinkman |first2=Bennett |title=Frantic Friday: Drummond files St. Isidore suit; Stitt fills state board, criticizes Sara Hill nomination |url=https://nondoc.com/2023/10/20/drummond-files-lawsuit-stitt-fills-state-board-criticizes-sara-hill-nomination/ |access-date=October 23, 2023 |work=NonDoc |date=October 20, 2023}}</ref> The governor's office denied requesting that the commission oppose Hill's nomination.<ref name="McNutt-1" /> Its communications director, Abegail Cave, said that Stitt will not support bills that lessen cockfighting penalties.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-03-18 |title=Raising roosters is big business. Now a push to ease penalties for cockfighting is ruffling feathers |url=https://www.kosu.org/2024-03-18/cockfighting-penalties-oklahoma-breeders |access-date=2024-03-26 |website=KOSU |language=en}}</ref> The Oklahoma Gamefowl Commission donated to Stitt, the Republican State House Committee of Oklahoma, and the Oklahoma Senate Republican State PAC.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hoffmann |first=Madeline |date=2025-06-18 |title=Video shows Oklahoma Gamefowl Commission leaders at illegal cockfights |url=https://nondoc.com/2025/06/18/video-shows-oklahoma-gamefowl-commission-leaders-at-illegal-cockfights/ |access-date=2025-06-18 |website=NonDoc |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Government reform===
In his first state of the state address, Stitt called for increased appointment power over major state agencies. The legislature granted his request by adopting five new laws, giving him direct control over the [[Oklahoma Department of Corrections]], the [[Oklahoma Health Care Authority]], the [[Oklahoma Department of Transportation]], the [[Oklahoma Office of Juvenile Affairs]], and the [[Oklahoma Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://kfor.com/2019/03/13/gov-stitt-signs-five-government-accountability-bills/|title=Stitt signs 5 government accountability bills|last=Zheng|first=Lili|date=March 13, 2019}}{{Dead link|date=January 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> These agencies were previously under the control of multi-member boards or commissions that acted independently of the governor.

In exchange for additional appointment powers and at legislative leaders' request, Stitt signed into law SB1, which established the Oklahoma Legislative Office of Fiscal Transparency in the legislative branch. Under the direction of an oversight committee composed of members of the [[Oklahoma State Senate|State Senate]] and [[Oklahoma House of Representatives|House of Representatives]], the office will provide auditing, evaluation, and investigative services for the legislature relating to the governor's proposed budget and expenditures by the executive branch.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.duncanbanner.com/oklahoma/column-legislature-adjourns-gov-stitt-signs-budget/article_3d568f1c-7ffb-11e9-832a-ef25267c86e7.html|title=Legislature adjourns, Gov. Stitt signs budget|last=Boles|first=Brad|date=May 26, 2019}}</ref>

===Guns===
The first law Stitt signed after taking office permitted anyone 21 or older, or 18 if a member or veteran of the [[United States Armed Forces]], to carry a firearm without obtaining a permit or completing training, after which gun deaths in Oklahoma increased by nearly 20% compared to the previous ten years.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/constitutional-carry-becomes-first-legislation-signed-into-law-by-gov/article_1c943003-1eca-542e-8d71-cfab83d93328.html|title='Constitutional carry' becomes first legislation signed into law by Gov. Kevin Stitt|last=Hoberock|first=Barbara|date=February 27, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Felder |first=Ben |title=As Oklahoma's gun access expanded, deaths from firearms increased |url=https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/2022/06/26/oklahoma-gun-deaths-jumped-after-stitt-signed-permitless-carry-law/7567774001/ |access-date=2025-08-14 |website=The Oklahoman |language=en-US}}</ref> Stitt also signed HB2010, which expands the places a firearm may be carried to include municipal zoos and parks, regardless of size, as long as it is concealed.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.oscn.net/applications/oscn/DeliverDocument.asp?CiteID=484186 |title=Oklahoma Firearms Act of 1971 Amendments Act |publisher=Oscn.net |date=2019-11-01 |access-date=2022-11-22}}</ref>

===Healthcare===
Stitt opposes Medicaid expansion in Oklahoma.<ref>{{cite news |last=Rowley |first=D. Sean |date=May 17, 2019 |title=Stitt not supportive of Medicaid expansion in Oklahoma |url=https://www.cherokeephoenix.org/Article/index/103205 |work=Cherokee Phoenix}}</ref> His refusal to expand the program resulted in the filing of an citizens' initiative petition, [[2020 Oklahoma Question 802|State Question 802]], to enact the expansion into the state constitution notwithstanding Stitt's opposition.

===Immigration===

While Stitt implemented Operation Guardian to aid President [[Deportation in the second presidency of Donald Trump|Trump's deportation efforts]]<ref>[https://oklahoma.gov/governor/newsroom/newsroom/2025/governor-stitt-rolls-out-plan-to-implement-operation-guardian.html Governor Stitt Rolls Out Plan to Implement Operation Guardian] February 5, 2025.</ref> in February 2025, he opposed the [[Oklahoma State Superintendent of Public Instruction|Oklahoma state superintendent of public instruction]]'s proposed mandate to request the citizenship status of children enrolling in public school,<ref>[https://apnews.com/article/oklahoma-immigration-schools-kevin-stitt-ryan-walters-ff55d7d2b61410cae933c3872b4b2393 Oklahoma governor says he’ll stop plan to collect kids’ immigration status]. February 13, 2025. Sean Murphy [[AP News]].</ref> saying that elementary-school children were not a threat to public safety. [[Ryan Walters (American politician)|Ryan Walters]], the state superintendent whom Stitt once appointed as the [[Oklahoma Secretary of Education|Oklahoma secretary of education]], responded that Stitt is part of the [[Deep state conspiracy theory in the United States|deep state]] against Trump.<ref>[https://www.aa.com.tr/en/americas/oklahoma-governor-rejects-proposal-asking-parents-to-provide-immigration-status-when-enrolling-children/3492673 Oklahoma governor rejects proposal asking parents to provide immigration status when enrolling children]. February 25, 2025. Seda Sevencan. [[Anadolu Agency]].</ref>

===Tribal relations===
Under the authority of the federal [[Indian Gaming Regulatory Act]], in 2004 Oklahoma voters approved State Question 712, which adopted the Oklahoma State-Tribal Gaming Act. Under the act, the State of Oklahoma offers each federally recognized Indian tribe the right to conduct commercial gambling within its territory upon accepting the terms of a uniform state-tribal gaming compact. The compact allowed the compacting tribes to conduct gaming in return for "exclusivity fees" to the state treasury averaging 6% of gaming revenues.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://stateimpact.npr.org/oklahoma/tag/tribal-gaming/|title= Tribal Gaming |last=National Public Radio}}</ref> The compact was scheduled to automatically renew on January 1, 2020.

In a July 2019 op-ed in the ''[[Tulsa World]]'' followed by a letter to the chiefs of 35 Oklahoma tribes, Stitt called on tribal leaders to renegotiate the terms of the compact before its expiration date.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://oklahoman.com/article/5635823/pushing-for-more-state-revenue-stitt-calls-for-renegotiating-oklahoma-tribal-gaming-compacts?|title=Stitt calls for renegotiating Oklahoma tribal gaming compacts|last=Forman|first=Carmen|date=July 10, 2019}}</ref> In particular, he called for increasing the exclusivity fees to between 13% and 25%.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/opinion/columnists/gov-kevin-stitt-new-gaming-compacts-must-protect-the-interests/article_ae5596f7-e9e5-5613-9bbb-c6341af9259f.html
|title=Gov. Kevin Stitt: New gaming compacts must protect the interests of the tribes and the state|last=Stitt|first=Kevin|date=July 8, 2019}}</ref> Stitt's office maintained the compact is not subject to automatic renewal, a claim the tribes rejected, believing it will continue indefinitely unless changes are mutually agreed upon.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://oklahoman.com/article/5636087/oklahomas-five-tribes-reject-stitts-call-to-renegotiate-gaming-compacts|title=Oklahoma's Five Tribes reject Stitt's call to renegotiate gaming compacts|last=Forman|first=Carmen|date=July 13, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/legal-expert-says-stitt-mistaken-on-tribal-gaming-fees-assessment/article_39984cdb-3bc1-52e4-aaf8-0b1f280b62fc.html|title=Legal expert says Stitt mistaken on tribal gaming fees assessment |last=Hoberock|first=Barbara|date=July 28, 2019}}</ref> In either event, the [[Oklahoma Legislature]] would presumably have to be involved in any renegotiation, since the state's compact offer is defined and controlled by state statute, and federal law requires that the [[United States Department of the Interior]] approve any new compact terms.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://oklahoman.com/article/5638406/lawmakers-urge-collaboration-in-gaming-compact-negotiations?|title=Lawmakers urge collaboration in gaming compact negotiations|last=Forman|first=Carmen|date=August 12, 2019}}</ref>

In August 2019, the various tribes refused to meet with Stitt to negotiate the amount of the exclusivity fees unless he conceded that the compact would otherwise automatically renew.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://oklahoman.com/article/5639891/tribes-to-stitt-we-still-have-some-fundamental-differencees-in-our-positions|title=Tribes to Stitt: 'We still have some fundamental differences in our positions'|last=Forman|first=Carmen|date=August 30, 2019}}</ref> Stitt had proposed a September 3 date to begin discussions but the tribes rejected it.

At the end of December 2019, the [[Choctaw Nation of Oklahoma|Choctaw]], [[Cherokee Nation|Cherokee]], and [[Chickasaw Nation|Chickasaw]] tribes filed suit in the [[United States District Court for the Western District of Oklahoma]] to end the dispute over the compact.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/communities/skiatook/news/cherokee-chickasaw-and-choctaw-nations-file-federal-lawsuit-to-end/article_6282e4ce-5ae5-5821-b2e2-abf5e4706949.html|title=Cherokee, Chickasaw and Choctaw Nations file federal lawsuit to end compact renewal dispute|author=<!--Not stated--> reports|website=Tulsa World|date=December 31, 2019 |language=en|access-date=2019-12-31}}</ref> On December 31, Stitt signed an extension to the hunting and fishing license compact with the Choctaw Nation, a previous point of contention.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://kfor.com/2019/12/31/choctaw-nation-hunting-fishing-compact-set-to-expire-without-word-from-governors-office/|title=Update: Gov. Stitt signs 1-year extension for Choctaw Nation's hunting, fishing compact|date=2019-12-31|website=KFOR.com|language=en|access-date=2019-12-31|archive-date=December 31, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191231161301/https://kfor.com/2019/12/31/choctaw-nation-hunting-fishing-compact-set-to-expire-without-word-from-governors-office/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On July 28, 2020, U.S. district judge [[Timothy D. DeGiusti]] ruled in the tribes' favor, holding that their compacts with the state automatically renewed for an additional 15-year term on January 1, 2020. A week earlier, on July 21, the Oklahoma Supreme Court ruled that the new gaming compacts signed by the state and the [[Comanche Nation]] and the [[Otoe-Missouria Tribe]] are invalid under state law. The Court ruled that Stitt "exceeded his authorities" in entering into the compacts because they would have allowed gaming that is illegal in Oklahoma, like [[sports betting]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Richards|first=Dillon|date=2020-07-28|title=Federal judge rules tribal gaming compacts automatically renewed at start of 2020|url=https://www.koco.com/article/federal-judge-rules-tribal-gaming-compacts-automatically-renewed-at-start-of-2020/33444984|access-date=2020-09-30|website=KOCO|language=en}}</ref>

On July 9, 2020, the [[United States Supreme Court]] decided in ''[[McGirt v. Oklahoma]]'' that half of the land of the state of Oklahoma made up of tribal nations like the Cherokee are officially Native American tribal land jurisdictions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2020/07/09/889562040/supreme-court-rules-that-about-half-of-oklahoma-is-indian-land|title = Supreme Court Rules That About Half of Oklahoma is Native American Land|website = NPR|date = July 9, 2020|last1 = Wamsley|first1 = Laurel}}</ref> Stitt, a Cherokee Nation citizen, sought to reverse the Supreme Court decision, but in 2021 Oklahoma could not block the federal action to grant the Cherokee Nation along with the Chickasaw, Choctaw, [[Muscogee Nation|Muscogee (Creek)]] and [[Seminole Nation of Oklahoma|Seminole]] Nations reservation status.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/oklahoma-governors-tribal-fight-raises-ancestry-questions-69306593|title=Oklahoma governor's tribal fight raises ancestry questions|website=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]}}</ref>

In May 2023, Stitt vetoed legislation that would have allowed students to wear tribal regalia during their graduation ceremonies,<ref>{{Cite web |agency=Associated Press |date=2023-05-17 |title=Native American former student sues Oklahoma school for removing feather at graduation |url=https://www.oregonlive.com/native-american-news/2023/05/native-american-former-student-sues-oklahoma-school-for-removing-feather-at-graduation.html |access-date=2023-05-18 |website=oregonlive |language=en}}</ref> but the state legislature overrode his veto.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/native-american-tribal-regalia-oklahoma-veto-override-418f81adba2937a2d0a4eecae2c2eede|title=Oklahoma Legislature overrides governor's veto of tribal regalia bill|website=[[Associated Press]]|date=May 25, 2023|access-date=August 10, 2024}}</ref>

In June 2023, Senator [[Greg Treat]] criticized senators who did not show up for a tribal compact vote to override one of Stitt's vetoes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=FERGUSON |first=TOM |date=2023-06-26 |title='Others will have to explain why they weren't here': Oklahoma Senate fails to override veto on tribal tobacco compact |url=https://okcfox.com/newsletter-daily/others-will-have-to-explain-why-they-werent-here-oklahoma-senate-fails-to-override-veto-on-tribal-tobacco-compact-greg-treat-pro-tem-gov-kevin-stitt-chief-cherokee-choctaw-gary-batton-chuck-hoskin-hb-1005x-sb-26x-politics-special-session-motor-vehicle |access-date=2023-06-30 |website=KOKH |language=en}}</ref> The next month, he called Stitt "ineffective" and said they were one vote shy of overriding.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2023-07-24 |title=Treat: Governor 'ineffective,' Oklahoma Senate must override his vetoes of compacts with tribes |url=https://www.publicradiotulsa.org/local-regional/2023-07-24/treat-governor-ineffective-oklahoma-senate-must-override-his-vetoes-of-compacts-with-tribes |access-date=2023-07-26 |website=Public Radio Tulsa |language=en}}</ref> When the override vote was called again, they got enough votes, but Stitt called it an "illegitimate process".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Weber |first=Andy |date=2023-07-25 |title=Senate votes to override Gov. Kevin Stitt's vetoes regarding compacts with tribes |url=https://www.koco.com/article/oklahoma-legislature-overrides-kevin-stitt-vetoes-tribal-compacts/44631107 |access-date=2023-07-26 |website=KOCO |language=en}}</ref> Tribal leaders applauded the override.<ref>{{Cite web |last=FERGUSON |first=TOM |date=2023-07-24 |title='He does not believe that we have sovereignty': Cherokee, Choctaw chiefs react to tribal compact veto overrides |url=https://okcfox.com/newsletter-daily/he-does-not-believe-that-we-have-sovereignty-cherokee-choctaw-chiefs-react-to-tribal-compact-veto-overrides-chuck-hoskin-jr-gary-batton-kevin-stitt-oklahoma-politics-governor-indian-country-mcgirt-tulsa-10th-circuit-court-appeals-citizen-tribes-native |access-date=2023-07-26 |website=KOKH |language=en}}</ref> In July 2020, a video Stitt made had a number of erroneous claims about Native American rights, such as that they do not have to obey the speed limit.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |last2= |date=2023-07-20 |title=Fact-checking Oklahoma Governor's claim that Indigenous, non-Indigenous drivers have different speed limits |url=https://www.kosu.org/local-news/2023-07-20/fact-checking-oklahoma-governors-claim-that-indigenous-non-indigenous-drivers-have-different-speed-limits |access-date=2023-07-26 |website=KOSU |language=en}}</ref>

Also, one day after the veto override, Attorney General [[Gentner Drummond]] entered a "federal lawsuit on behalf of the state" originally brought by Stitt against the U.S. Department of Interior and four tribal nations. Drummond claimed Stitt was "betraying his duties to the state and wasting taxpayer money on private law firms" and that the compacts Stitt signed "with the [[Comanche|Comanche Nation]], the [[Otoe–Missouria Tribe of Indians|Otoe-Missouria Tribe]], the [[United Keetoowah Band of Cherokee Indians]], and the [[Kialegee Tribal Town]] are invalid because he signed the compacts without first getting legislative approval for expanded types of gaming listed within them, including sports betting."<ref>{{Cite web |last=McNutt |first=Michael |date=2023-07-25 |title='An extraordinary act': Drummond enters lawsuit over tribal gaming compacts |url=https://nondoc.com/2023/07/25/drummond-lawsuit-tribal-gaming-compacts/ |access-date=2023-07-26 |website=NonDoc |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Response to disasters and emergencies ===

==== Coronavirus outbreak ====
In March 2020, Stitt went out to restaurants amid the [[COVID-19 pandemic|coronavirus pandemic]] and posted a photo on [[Twitter]] of him doing so with two of his children.<ref>{{Citation|title=Collins pushes Donald Trump on Devin Nunes' coronavirus comments – CNN Video|date=March 16, 2020 |url=https://www.cnn.com/videos/politics/2020/03/16/collins-trump-nunes-coronavirus-guidelines-sot-vpx.cnn|access-date=2020-09-30}}</ref><ref name=":9">{{cite web|url=https://www.motherjones.com/coronavirus-updates/2020/03/devin-nunes-this-is-why-we-cant-have-nice-things-coronavirus/|title=Devin Nunes just went on Fox News and told viewers, "It's a great time to go out"|last=Rosenberg|first=Jacob|website=Mother Jones|language=en-US|access-date=2020-03-15}}</ref> He later deleted the tweet, and his spokesperson said, "the governor will continue to take his family out to dinner and to the grocery store without living in fear and encourages Oklahomans to do the same."<ref>{{cite web|last=Budryk|first=Zack|date=2020-03-16|title=Oklahoma governor will continue to 'take his family out to dinner' amid pandemic|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/487846-oklahoma-governor-will-continue-to-take-his-family-out-to-dinner-amid|access-date=2020-09-30|website=The Hill|language=en}}</ref> President Trump said he did not advocate going out to eat but did not criticize Stitt.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/local/government-and-politics/president-trump-on-gov-stitt-s-deleted-tweet-probably-better/article_394a41e7-4b28-5ed0-a764-bfc0adb9bf3c.html|title=President Trump on Gov. Stitt's deleted tweet: Probably better not to go to restaurants|work=The Oklahoman|publisher=Tulsa World|date=16 March 2020|last=Casteel|first=Chris|access-date=7 April 2020}}</ref> In the tweet, Stitt wrote, "Eating with my kids and all my fellow Oklahomans ... It's packed tonight!" The photograph he posted with his kids showed them smiling while surrounded by restaurant patrons.<ref>{{cite news |last=Douglas |first=Kaylee |date=March 15, 2020 |title=Oklahoma governor, mayor face backlash after posting photos in crowded restaurants |url=https://kfor.com/news/local/ok-governor-okc-mayor-face-backlash-after-posting-photos-in-crowded-restaurants-amid-coronavirus-concerns/ |url-status=live |work=Oklahoma News 4 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200605190833/https://kfor.com/news/local/ok-governor-okc-mayor-face-backlash-after-posting-photos-in-crowded-restaurants-amid-coronavirus-concerns/ |archive-date=June 5, 2020 |access-date=July 17, 2020 }}</ref> On June 20, Stitt attended the [[2020 Trump Tulsa rally|Trump rally in Tulsa]], and was seen without wearing a mask.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wu |first1=Nicholas |last2=Subramanian |first2=Courtney |date=July 15, 2020 |title=Oklahoma Gov. Kevin Stitt tests positive for coronavirus |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2020/07/15/oklahoma-gov-stitt-tests-positive-covid-weeks-after-trump-rally/3287401001/ |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=July 16, 2020 }}</ref> On July 15, Stitt announced that he had tested positive for [[Coronavirus disease 2019|COVID-19]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Murphy |first1=Sean |title=Oklahoma's governor says he has tested positive for COVID-19 |url=https://www.moultonadvertiser.com/news/national/article_c9354339-2a21-5fe1-851c-dc07151d5eb7.html |work=The Moulton Advertiser |agency=Associated Press |date=July 15, 2020 |language=en |access-date=July 15, 2020 |archive-date=July 16, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200716093756/https://www.moultonadvertiser.com/news/national/article_c9354339-2a21-5fe1-851c-dc07151d5eb7.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> He was the first United States governor diagnosed with COVID-19.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Oklahoma's Kevin Stitt is the first governor to test positive for the coronavirus|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2020/07/15/oklahoma-kevin-stitt-coronavirus/|access-date=2020-09-30}}</ref>

In April 2020, Stitt ordered a massive purchase of [[hydroxychloroquine]], a drug of unproven efficacy as a treatment against the coronavirus but which had been heavily promoted by Donald Trump and his allies.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|title=Oklahoma trying to return its $2m stockpile of hydroxychloroquine|url=https://www.readfrontier.org/stories/oklahoma-trying-to-return-its-2m-stockpile-of-hydroxychloroquine/|access-date=2021-01-27|website=The Frontier|language=en-US}}</ref> By January 2021, Oklahoma had a $2 million stockpile of hydroxychloroquine which it sought to offload.<ref name=":0" />

On July 30, 2021, ''[[Oklahoma Watch]]'' released a review of Stitt's [[Twitter]] since he received the [[COVID-19 vaccine]] and found he posted the least on social media to encourage vaccination of all the governors of states surrounding Oklahoma, including [[Colorado]], [[Kansas]], [[Missouri]], [[Arkansas]], [[Texas]], and [[New Mexico]]. Only 1.53%, or 3 out of 193, of Stitt's tweets encouraged COVID-19 vaccination. It also found that Stitt had not used his [[Facebook]] account to encourage vaccination in months and that none of his last 45 press releases were about vaccination, at a time when Oklahoma had one of the highest COVID-19 test positivity rates in the country.<ref name="VaccinationJuly2021">{{cite news |last1=Brown |first1=Trevor |title=As Other Governors Pull Out Vaccine Media Blitz, Oklahoma's Stitt Largely Silent |url=https://oklahomawatch.org/2021/07/30/as-other-governors-pull-out-vaccine-media-blitz-oklahomas-stitt-largely-silent/ |access-date=1 August 2021 |publisher=[[Oklahoma Watch]] |date=30 July 2021}}</ref>

Stitt sent U.S. defense secretary [[Lloyd Austin]] a letter requesting that COVID-19 vaccine requirements for the Oklahoma National Guard be suspended.<ref name=":2">{{cite web|last=World|first=Barbara Hoberock Tulsa|title=State adjutant general says he was relieved of his duties by Gov. Stitt|url=https://tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/state-adjutant-general-says-he-was-relieved-of-his-duties-by-gov-stitt/article_790f1450-433c-11ec-baa0-636951325a47.html|access-date=2021-11-14|website=Tulsa World|date=November 12, 2021 |language=en}}</ref> Stitt subsequently fired the commander of the Oklahoma National Guard because the commander had advocated for his troops to be vaccinated.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news|date=2021|title=Oklahoma National Guard rejects Pentagon's coronavirus vaccine mandate|newspaper=Washington Post|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2021/11/13/oklahoma-national-guard-vaccine-mandate/}}</ref><ref name=":2" /> Stitt's new appointee refused to implement the COVID-19 vaccine requirements.<ref>{{cite web|last=Winkie|first=Davis|date=2021-11-12|title=Oklahoma Guard goes rogue, rejects COVID vaccine mandate after sudden change of command|url=https://www.armytimes.com/news/your-army/2021/11/12/oklahoma-guard-goes-rogue-rejects-covid-vaccine-mandate-after-sudden-change-of-command/|access-date=2021-11-14|website=Army Times|language=en}}</ref><ref name=":3" />

==== Natural disasters ====
In June 2023, after severe storms hit parts of Oklahoma with hurricane-force winds and tornadic activity that knocked out power for days for more than 100,000 energy customers during severe heat waves,<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Sandoval |first1=Edgar |last2=Jones |first2=Judson |date=2023-06-21 |title=Extreme Heat and More Storms Threaten an Already Battered Oklahoma |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/21/us/oklahoma-heat-power.html |access-date=2023-06-23 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> [[Tulsa, Oklahoma|Tulsa]] Mayor [[G. T. Bynum|G.T. Bynum]] requested that Stitt announce a state of emergency, but Stitt did not respond to Bynum's calls.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Osborne |first=Deon |date=2023-06-20 |title=Gov. Stitt delays declaring state of emergency: Process or politics? |url=http://theblackwallsttimes.com/2023/06/20/gov-stitt-delays-declaring-state-of-emergency-process-or-politics/ |access-date=2023-06-24 |website=[[The Black Wall Street Times]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2023-06-22 |title=Mayor Bynum hasn't heard from Gov. Stitt, Lt. Gov. Pinnell return to state Thursday |url=https://www.kjrh.com/news/local-news/lt-governor-pinnell-returning-to-oklahoma-on-thursday-left-state-monday-afternoon |access-date=2023-06-24 |website=2 News Oklahoma KJRH Tulsa |language=en}}</ref> Days later, [[Senate pro tem|Senate president pro tempore]] [[Greg Treat]] was informed he was the acting governor and could declare a state of emergency, which he eventually did.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ferguson |first=Kevin Severin, Tom |date=2023-06-20 |title=Pro Tem Treat signs executive order within hour of finding out he's acting governor |url=https://okcfox.com/news/local/oklahoma-city-senate-pro-tem-greg-treat-executive-order-state-emergency-governors-office-power-kevin-stitt-reviewing-documents-tulsa-mayor-gt-bynum-matt-pinnell-exective-order-acting-storms-damage-devastation |access-date=2023-06-23 |website=KOKH |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Citation |last=Cashman |first=Shelby |title=Sen. Greg Treat addresses disaster response, role as acting governor |date=2023-06-22 |url=https://www.koco.com/article/oklahoma-tulsa-senator-greg-treat-acting-governor-disaster-response/44302581 |access-date=2023-06-23 |language=en}}</ref> Stitt was in Paris and Lieutenant Governor [[Matt Pinnell]] was also out of state.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2023-06-22 |title=Treat: Oklahoma Legislature 'stands ready' to help in Tulsa's storm recovery |url=https://www.publicradiotulsa.org/local-regional/2023-06-21/treat-legislature-ready-to-help-tulsa |access-date=2023-06-23 |website=Public Radio Tulsa |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2023-06-19 |title=Tulsa officials hopeful for State of Emergency declaration |url=https://www.kjrh.com/news/local-news/tulsa-officials-hopeful-for-state-of-emergency-declaration |access-date=2023-06-23 |website=2 News Oklahoma KJRH Tulsa |language=en}}</ref> Six days after the event, Stitt said he was in contact with Bynum<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2023-06-23 |title=Gov. Kevin Stitt visits Tulsa Friday morning, talked to 2 News |url=https://www.kjrh.com/news/local-news/gov-kevin-stitt-visits-tulsa-friday-morning-talked-to-2-news |access-date=2023-06-24 |website=2 News Oklahoma KJRH Tulsa |language=en}}</ref> and Bynum tried to redirect attention from the story.<ref>{{Citation |title=Bynum addresses Stitt's comments | date=June 23, 2023 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zporCjEIjAE |access-date=2023-06-24 |language=en}}</ref> At least three people died from the storms.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Withrow |first=Brooke |date=2023-06-20 |title=Third person confirmed dead after severe storms hit Oklahoma |url=https://www.koco.com/article/oklahoma-tulsa-storms-third-person-dead/44273013 |access-date=2023-06-24 |website=KOCO |language=en}}</ref>

===Sports betting===
In January 2023, Stitt announced his support for legalizing [[sports betting]] in Oklahoma after Representative [[Ken Luttrell]] filed a bill to allow [[federally recognized tribes]] in the state to offer sports betting.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Love |first1=Ryan |title=Oklahoma governor in support of legalizing sports betting |url=https://www.kjrh.com/sports/oklahoma-governor-in-support-of-legalizing-sports-betting |access-date=4 January 2023 |work=[[KOCO]] |date=3 January 2023}}</ref>

===Judicial reform and appointments===
Stitt signed legislation reorganizing the [[Oklahoma Supreme Court]] and the [[Oklahoma Court of Civil Appeals]]. Before the reforms, Supreme Court justices were appointed from nine separate districts representing various collections of counties. Under the legislation, as of 2020 the Court's nine judicial districts were redrawn such that five were made coequal with the state's five congressional districts and the other four are at large with the state as whole.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hoberock |first=Barbara |date=April 26, 2019 |title=Stitt signs bill to reorganize Oklahoma Supreme Court, Court of Criminal Appeals |url=https://www.tulsaworld.com/news/state-and-regional/stitt-signs-bill-to-reorganize-oklahoma-supreme-court-court-of/article_ec563fc7-9362-5f9b-b0fb-c035f96bc901.html}}</ref> Similarly, the five judicial districts used to appoint judges to the Court of Criminal Appeals were made coequal with the congressional districts. The legislation left the method for appointing appellate judges via the [[Oklahoma Judicial Nominating Commission]] unchanged. The reform's ostensible purpose was to increase the pool of applicants to the appellate courts.

The governor of Oklahoma is responsible for making appointments to [[Courts of Oklahoma|Oklahoma state courts]] upon a vacancy. Candidates for appointment are reviewed by the [[Oklahoma Judicial Nominating Commission]], which forwards three names to the governor. The governor appoints one of the three without further confirmation. As of 2020, there are 29 appellate court judges (nine Supreme Court justices, five Court of Criminal Appeals judge, 12 Court of Civil Appeals judges, and three Court of Military Appeals judges) and 156 trial judges (75 district judges, 77 associate district judges, and four Workers Compensation Court judges) subject to the gubernatorial appointment process.

